Wait, but there's a subtle point: after a delimiter, the first letter of the next word — for example "I" in "Something - I made up" — is uppercase, so it gets pushed. But what about a lowercase word after a delimiter, like "made"? "m" — prev is a space, is_word_start is true, m is alphabetic → push. Good.

But what about "It's" — the apostrophe: prev is 't' (alphabetic), so the apostrophe is not a word start. The 's' after the apostrophe: prev is the apostrophe (non-alphabetic) → word start → 's' gets pushed! This is wrong: "First In, First Out" — "First" → F, then comma+space, then "First" → F, "Out" → O. That's fine. But "It's Friday" → "It" gives I, then apostrophe, then 's' after the apostrophe → word start → 's' gets pushed → "IFS" instead of "IF".

Hmm, but the test "apostrophes" is "Halley's Comet" → "HC". "Halley's": H is pushed, "alley" is lowercase, apostrophe, then 's' — prev is the apostrophe (non-alphabetic) → word start → 's' gets pushed → "HSC" ≠ "HC".

So treating apostrophes as word separators is wrong. Per the spec: hyphens are word separators, all other punctuation is removed. So apostrophes should be removed and not treated as separators — "It's" is one word "Its".

So the correct model is: remove all punctuation except hyphens, and treat hyphens and whitespace as separators. Then take the first letter of each word, plus camelCase bumps.

But note: "Complementary metal-oxide semiconductor" → CMOS. Removing the hyphen as a separator: "Complementary metal oxide semiconductor" → CMOS. Good.

What about "The Road _Not_ Taken"? Underscore — "all other punctuation can be removed". If we remove underscores: "The Road Not Taken" → TRNT. Good.

So the implementation: iterate over characters, skipping non-alphabetic characters except hyphens, and treat hyphens and whitespace as separators.

```rust
pub fn abbreviate(phrase: &str) -> String {
    let mut acronym = String::new();
    let mut prev: Option<char> = None;

    for c in phrase.chars() {
        if c.is_alphabetic() {
            let is_word_start = prev.map_or(true, |p| !p.is_alphabetic());
            let is_camel_hump = c.is_uppercase() && prev.map_or(false, |p| p.is_lowercase());
            if is_word_start || is_camel_hump {
                acronym.push(c.to_ascii_uppercase());
            }
            prev = Some(c);
        } else if !c.is_whitespace() {
            // Remove punctuation; hyphens act as separators
            if c == '-' {
                prev = Some(' ');
            } else {
                prev = None;
            }
        }
        // Whitespace: prev stays as-is? No...
    }
}
```

Hmm, wait. Let me think about what prev should be after a separator. If prev is a separator (whitespace or hyphen), then the next alphabetic character is a word start. If prev is None... the first character is a word start.

But there's a problem: after removing an apostrophe (setting prev = None), the next character 's' in "It's" — prev is None → word start → gets pushed. Wrong!

So the semantics of None is the problem. Let me use a different approach: track "last was a separator" as a bool, and track the last alphabetic character separately.

```rust
pub fn abbreviate(phrase: &str) -> String {
    let mut acronym = String::new();
    let mut last_alpha: Option<char> = None;
    let mut at_word_start = true;

    for c in phrase.chars() {
        if c.is_alphabetic() {
            let is_camel_hump = c.is_uppercase() && last_alpha.map_or(false, |p| p.is_lowercase());
            if at_word_start || is_camel_hump {
                acronym.push(c.to_ascii_uppercase());
            }
            last_alpha = Some(c);
            at_word_start = false;
        } else {
            // Whitespace and hyphens are separators; other punctuation is removed
            at_word_start = c.is_whitespace() || c == '-';
        }
    }

    acronym
}
```

